      <!-- H2 2: Mechanics of Line Endings -->
      <div class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
          The Mechanics of Line Endings: LF (\n) vs CRLF (\r\n) vs CR (\r)
        </h2>
        <p class="text-sm sm:text-base text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
          Digital text represents line breaks using non-printing control characters inherited from mechanical typewriters:
        </p>
        <ul class="space-y-2 text-sm sm:text-base text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 list-disc pl-5">
          <li><strong>Unix &amp; Linux (LF - <code class="text-xs font-mono bg-slate-100 dark:bg-slate-800 px-1 py-0.5 rounded text-purple-600 dark:text-purple-400">\n</code>):</strong> Line Feed moves the cursor down one line. Used universally in macOS, Linux, Android, and modern web protocols.</li>
          <li><strong>Windows (CRLF - <code class="text-xs font-mono bg-slate-100 dark:bg-slate-800 px-1 py-0.5 rounded text-purple-600 dark:text-purple-400">\r\n</code>):</strong> Carriage Return returns the cursor to the left margin, followed by Line Feed. Used across Windows Notepad and legacy DOS tools.</li>
          <li><strong>Classic Mac OS (CR - <code class="text-xs font-mono bg-slate-100 dark:bg-slate-800 px-1 py-0.5 rounded text-purple-600 dark:text-purple-400">\r</code>):</strong> Carriage Return only, seen occasionally in older text archives.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="text-sm sm:text-base text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
          FastToolVerse uses generalized regular expressions (<code class="text-xs font-mono bg-slate-100 dark:bg-slate-800 px-1 py-0.5 rounded text-purple-600 dark:text-purple-400">/\r\n|\r|\n/g</code>) to capture and standardize all line-ending conventions seamlessly, preventing phantom characters or mismatched encoding bugs.
